"""Editable English submission copy. Facts frozen to the 18 October 2026 baseline."""
TITLE='PhaseAtlas: From PDE Animations to Reproducible Numerical Evidence'
AUTHOR='Niu Te | Imperial College London'
DATE='18 October 2026 | Pre-event project snapshot'
def p(t):return ('p',t)
def h(t):return ('h',t)
def img(name,caption):return ('image',name,caption)
def table(rows):return ('table',rows)

ONE_PAGE=[
 h('The problem'),p('An attractive PDE animation can conceal a wrong boundary condition, inadequate resolution or a misleading interpretation of a travelling wave. Researchers need to inspect the calculation behind a figure and give someone else enough information to reproduce it.'),
 h('The contribution'),p('PhaseAtlas is a browser-based scientific workbench that connects editable PDE models to animated experiments, numerical checks and portable evidence. The workflow combines three-level space/time studies, travelling-wave motion and deformation diagnostics, and parameter maps whose cells retain their trajectories. Supported second-order bounded models include Dirichlet, Neumann and passive Robin conditions; 1D fourth-order models accept two configurable linear boundary conditions at each end.'),
 img('verification','Executed benchmarks: Robin exact-mode errors decrease with refinement (left); measured thermal-content change tracks integrated model input for a cooling rod (right). The latter also depends on saved-frame quadrature.'),
 h('Demonstrated result and practical value'),p('For u_t = 0.1 u_xx on [-1,1], the Robin eigenmode test at t = 0.2 gives RMS errors of 4.863e-5, 1.250e-5 and 3.148e-6 on 16, 32 and 64 cells; the three-level observed order is 1.99995. All 90 current mathematical and numerical tests pass. A dimensionless cooling example relates wall exchange and diffusivity to mean temperature, while the MAE03 fourth-order front connects the workflow to nonlinear-wave research. These examples help users design comparisons, identify numerical artifacts and exchange complete experiments.'),
 h('What another researcher receives'),p('Equation and boundary editors; 1D/2D visualization for supported models; animation replay; refinement and boundary diagnostics; finite-time parameter scans; JSON/CSV/figure exports; and an independent Python reference. Computation runs locally in browser workers. No account or runtime LLM API key is required.'),
 h('Scope and provenance'),p('This is independently implemented, AI-assisted scientific software. It does not solve arbitrary PDEs or establish a new stability theorem. The cooling example is a model study, not an experimentally calibrated engineering prediction. The earlier MAE03 research and this 18 October software snapshot are pre-event work; any October 21-22 contribution must be documented separately.'),
 p('<b>Project:</b> https://github.com/NIU-55383/PhaseAtlas<br/><b>Companion:</b> full technical article, data, scripts and numerical methods in the submission package.')
]

ARTICLE=[
 [h('Abstract'),p('PhaseAtlas addresses a recurring problem in computational PDE research: visual accessibility is often separated from the evidence needed to assess a result. We present an independently implemented browser workbench that connects model editing, animated simulation, controlled refinement, travelling-wave diagnostics and finite-time parameter maps. The system retains configurations, boundary conditions and trajectories with each experiment and recomputes imported diagnostics from numerical arrays. A Robin heat-equation eigenmode exhibits observed spatial order 1.99995 in a fixed-time-step experiment. A boundary-cooling study demonstrates a practical comparison of diffusivity and heat exchange; a fourth-order front from the author\'s MAE03 research supplies a mathematical case study. The contribution is an integrated, inspectable software workflow. Numerical observations, same-mesh reference agreement and mathematical theorems are kept distinct.'),
 h('1. Research problem and intended users'),p('A smooth animation is a useful starting point for understanding a PDE, but it is not evidence that the discretization resolves that equation. A changed boundary condition can change the scientific problem. Grid error can mimic growth. Translational drift can inflate the difference between two otherwise similar wave profiles. The researcher must ask which quantity was measured, what was held fixed, and whether another implementation reproduces the observation.'),p('The primary users are PDE researchers, scientific-computing developers and research instructors. PhaseAtlas is intended to reduce the repeated work of building a small experimental interface, adding diagnostics and packaging a reproducible comparison. No user study has yet measured a time saving. The current evidence supports technical feasibility and reusable numerical workflows.'),
 h('2. Position relative to existing tools'),p('VisualPDE already provides accessible interactive PDE simulation and published validation examples [1]. Dedalus provides a substantial framework for solving equation-based spectral problems [2]. PhaseAtlas does not claim to invent browser simulation, refinement studies or parameter scans, and no performance or accuracy superiority over those tools is established. Its present emphasis is the combination of an inspectable experiment record, wave-specific diagnostics and metric-defined parameter maps in one small browser workflow. The implementation used here was developed independently; it does not incorporate VisualPDE source code or assets.'),
 h('Workflow'),table([['Define','Compute','Inspect','Exchange'],['Equations, initial data, domain and boundary rows','Workers retain numerical time histories','Resolution, balance, motion, shape and parameter response','Model, arrays, settings and method identifiers']])],

 [h('3. Numerical scope and implementation'),p('The Studio supports one to three fields on 1D intervals or 2D rectangles, subject to backend restrictions. A restricted expression parser accepts mathematical expressions rather than executable JavaScript. The browser uses CPU Float64 arithmetic and worker execution. The server hosts assets and health endpoints; it is not a remote scientific-computing cluster.'),table([['Backend','Supported setting','Principal restrictions'],['Periodic','1D/2D Fourier spatial derivatives; ETDRK4 integration','Extracted constant diagonal linear operators; explicit supported remainder; resolution limits'],['Second-order bounded','1D/2D cell-centered differences and SBDF2','Constant nonnegative diagonal diffusion; Dirichlet, Neumann or passive Robin data'],['Fourth-order bounded','1D cell-centered stencils and SBDF2','Negative constant leading fourth coefficient per field; two admissible linear rows per end']]),p('For the second-order backend, the stiff diagonal diffusion is implicit and supported remaining terms are explicit. Nonlinear advection is not shock capturing. In the fourth-order backend, constant same-field linear derivative terms are implicit; boundary traces are reconstructed from nearby cells and two specified linear rows. A singular reconstruction or failed solve is rejected. An admissible discrete boundary matrix is not, by itself, a proof that an arbitrary continuum boundary problem is well posed.'),
 h('3.1 Boundary values are part of the experiment'),p('The Robin convention is alpha*u + beta*d_n u = g, where d_n is the outward normal derivative. At the left endpoint d_n = -d_x; at the right d_n = d_x. The current Robin backend requires finite nonnegative constant/parameter alpha and beta, not both zero; g may depend on position, time and parameters. These restrictions support the implemented passive diffusion solve. General active Robin feedback is not claimed.'),p('At a cell center U a distance h/2 from a boundary, the reconstructed normal derivative is q = (g - alpha*U)/(beta + alpha*h/2). The resulting face input is included in the discrete diffusion operator. Taking alpha = 0 or beta = 0 recovers the supported Neumann or Dirichlet closure after scaling. The fourth-order editor separately allows linear combinations of u, d_n u, d_n^2 u and d_n^3 u, with two independent rows per end.'),
 h('3.2 Evidence cannot be inferred from a small residual alone'),p('A small discrete boundary residual records enforcement of the chosen boundary rows. A small algebraic balance closure records consistency of assembled terms. Neither measures error against the continuous PDE. PhaseAtlas therefore exposes exact-solution comparisons, controlled refinement and an independent Python implementation alongside these internal checks.')],

 [h('4. Case study I: verified boundary cooling'),p('The verification problem is u_t = nu*u_xx on [-1,1], with nu = 0.1, initial data cos(x), and Robin data tan(1)*u + d_n u = 0 at both ends. Its exact solution is exp(-0.1*t)*cos(x). Three independent runs use N = 16, 32 and 64 cell centers, final time 0.2 and time step 0.0002. Space is refined while time step, equation and boundary data are held fixed.'),
 table([['Cells N','Final RMS error to exact solution'],['16','4.8629086e-5'],['32','1.2504878e-5'],['64','3.1475601e-6']]),p('Adjacent-grid differences are compared on a common coarse grid. Their ratio gives p = log(D_01/D_12)/log(2) = 1.999945875. The exact errors and observed order are consistent with second-order behavior in this tested regime. They do not certify every equation, boundary combination or parameter setting accepted by the editor.'),
 img('verification','Figure 1. Left: exact-mode error under spatial refinement. Right: a separate cooling experiment, whose thermal-content change is compared with integrated input. Both plots use executed numerical output.'),
 h('4.1 A physically interpretable application'),p('A second experiment starts from u = 1 with surrounding value 0.2. It uses u_t = nu*u_xx and Bi*u + d_n u = Bi*0.2, where Bi is a dimensionless boundary-exchange coefficient. With nu = 0.1, Bi = 2, N = 64, time step 0.001 and final time 3, the computed mean is 0.74485847. The initial uniform field is incompatible with the suddenly applied cooling boundary at t = 0; this is explicitly treated as a start-up transient.'),p('For constant volumetric heat capacity, the integral of temperature is proportional to thermal content. The audit compares its change with the saved-time integral of boundary and bulk input. With 121 saved frames, the maximum absolute discrepancy is 8.711e-5 in dimensionless integral units. This combines numerical error and trapezoidal quadrature of saved output; it is not a calibrated energy-error tolerance. Physical prediction would require a specified material, dimensions, units, coefficients and experimental validation.')],

 [h('5. From one run to a useful parameter question'),p('A researcher may ask how much of a hot sample remains above ambient after a fixed time, and whether internal diffusion or surface exchange limits cooling. The workbench evaluates a user-selected expression and spatial statistic, then applies a temporal reduction such as final value, time average, maximum or first threshold event. Each map cell retains the experiment used to compute that quantity.'),
 img('parameter-map','Figure 2. Final mean temperature at t = 3 for a 4-by-4 scan of diffusivity and boundary exchange. Every cell is an independent initial-value run from u = 1, using N = 32 and time step 0.001. Lower values represent more cooling in this model.'),
 p('The sampled final means range from 0.30895041 to 0.94776467. The map exposes a response that a single animation does not: changing either diffusion or wall exchange changes the finite-time average, and the response depends on both parameters. These are finite-grid model outputs, not a newly discovered physical law. A decision near a threshold must be checked with finer spatial grids, smaller time steps and denser output sampling.'),
 h('Missing evidence stays missing'),p('Canceled and failed cells remain visible. An unobserved threshold event is missing rather than zero. An ambiguous wave crossing does not produce a fabricated speed. Finite-difference parameter sensitivities are reported as local secants, with one-sided treatment at sampled edges and unavailable values when necessary neighbors are missing. The interface does not relabel this finite-time scan as continuation of stationary solutions or a bifurcation diagram.'),
 h('Practical reuse'),p('The exported model can be adapted to another supported diffusion or reaction problem; trajectories and metric definitions can be inspected without rerunning it. A collaborator can replay a chosen cell, change the observation window and repeat the numerical study. These capabilities support model exploration and teaching. Application-specific clinical, industrial or safety decisions are outside the validated scope of this demonstration.')],

 [h('6. Case study II: a fourth-order travelling front'),p('The mathematical case comes from the author\'s earlier MAE03 investigation and the travelling-wave literature [3,4]. The equation is u_t = gamma*u_xxxx - beta*u_xxx + alpha*u_xx - d_x(a*u^2 + 2*b*u^3/3). The reference parameters are gamma = -1, beta = -15/2, alpha = -71/4, a = 0, b = 315/16 and speed c = 105/8. The profile v(xi) = (1 + exp(-xi))^(-3/2), xi = x - c*t, connects 0 to 1. Its strictly positive base makes the half-integer power real throughout the line.'),
 img('wave','Figure 3. A localized disturbance in the original front laboratory. Left: w/0.001 on one fixed color scale. Right: global, local and exponentially weighted finite-domain L2 norms, each divided by its own initial value. These are distinct observations, not a stability classification.'),
 p('The plotted nonlinear perturbation uses N = 2049, interval [-24,24], final time 0.06, time step 0.000025, amplitude 0.001 and 121 saved frames. The original laboratory evolves a perturbation equation with homogeneous clamped perturbation conditions. Its zero-perturbation state is well balanced by construction and is not an independent test of solver accuracy.'),
 h('Motion, deformation and boundaries'),p('In the general workbench, threshold tracking measures motion, while windowed phase alignment helps separate displacement from shape change. Global, local and weighted raw-difference norms answer different questions. The supplied reference formula need not be an exact solution of a perturbed experiment. Weighted decay, short-time growth or a finite-domain trace alone does not establish nonlinear orbital or convective stability.'),p('The newer general fourth-order Studio evolves the full field with user-specified endpoint rows. It is a different numerical path from the original perturbation laboratory and can drift even at zero added perturbation. Users must first measure that baseline drift, then vary grid, time step and domain. Prescribed finite boundaries are not absorbing whole-line conditions.')],

 [h('7. Reproducibility and evidence status'),p('The case-data generator writes complete trajectories, a numerical summary and SHA-256 hashes of the numerical source files. These hashes identify the files used; they do not authenticate an arbitrary imported trajectory. The evidence viewer validates structure and recomputes comparisons from arrays rather than trusting saved conclusions. Study import restores recipes and settings without automatically accepting numerical claims.'),p('The 18 October snapshot passes 90 mathematical and numerical tests. Coverage includes parser rejection, Fourier signs, Dirichlet/Neumann/Robin closures, fourth-order boundary rows, manufactured solutions, refinement, conservation behavior, missing-data handling and wave diagnostics. Tests provide targeted regression evidence, not universal certification. The latest preceding browser QA also exercised 30 workbench/Robin scenarios; presentation changes are checked separately.'),p('An independent NumPy/SciPy BDF run for the Robin mode at N = 32, final time 0.3 and browser time step 0.0002 gives a final relative L2 difference of 2.174e-10. The Python and browser implementations use the same spatial closure, so this is a same-mesh implementation/time-integration comparison. The exact-mode refinement on page 3 addresses spatial error separately. The reference input and report are included in the package.'),
 h('8. Contribution, limitations and next evaluation'),p('The current contribution is the connected scientific-software workflow and its explicit experiment records. The project does not claim a universal PDE solver, numerical superiority over established software, autonomous theorem discovery or a new mathematical theorem. General geometries, unrestricted variable-coefficient operators, 2D nonperiodic fourth-order problems, GPU acceleration and remote job execution remain outside this snapshot.'),p('The next evaluation should ask researchers to reproduce and modify a benchmark, compare results across implementations and document failures. A useful study would measure task completion, interpretation mistakes and reproducibility, rather than count interface features. A later mathematical paper requires a distinct new result and proof. This software article does not substitute for that work.'),
 h('Provenance and event status'),p('Author: Niu Te, Imperial College London. Development was AI-assisted, including coding, tests and documentation. The scientific computation does not call an external LLM at runtime. Earlier mathematical work and this dated software snapshot precede the October 21-22 event; only separately recorded event-period changes should be described as hackathon contributions. This manuscript is a project report prepared before the event, not a peer-reviewed paper.'),
 h('References and access'),p('[1] VisualPDE, Validating VisualPDE. <link href="https://visualpde.com/numerical-methods/validating-VisualPDE.html">visualpde.com/numerical-methods/validating-VisualPDE.html</link><br/>[2] Dedalus Project, About. <link href="https://dedalus-project.org/about/">dedalus-project.org/about/</link><br/>[3] Y. Hu and Q. Liu (2019), On traveling wave solutions of a class of KdV-Burgers-Kuramoto type equations. AIMS Mathematics 4, 1450-1465. <link href="https://doi.org/10.3934/math.2019.5.1450">doi:10.3934/math.2019.5.1450</link><br/>[4] M. A. Johnson, G. Lyng and C. Smith, On the Dynamics of Traveling Fronts Arising in Nanoscale Pattern Formation. <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.02233">arXiv:1810.02233</link><br/>Project source: <link href="https://github.com/NIU-55383/PhaseAtlas">github.com/NIU-55383/PhaseAtlas</link>. Numerical case files and generation scripts accompany this report. Web references checked 18 October 2026.')]
]

SCRIPT4=[
 ('0:00-0:35 | The problem','Homepage: show the real cooling animation.','A PDE animation can look convincing even when its boundary conditions or numerical resolution are wrong. I built PhaseAtlas to connect the picture to the evidence behind it. It is a browser workbench for researchers who want to edit a model, inspect its dynamics and share a calculation that another person can check.'),
 ('0:35-1:15 | A practical question','Cooling case: open its model in the workbench, run and play.','Start with a hot rod exchanging heat with its surroundings. The Robin boundary condition represents that exchange. I can change the diffusion coefficient or the exchange coefficient, run the equation and inspect the temperature through time. The model, initial data and boundary conditions remain visible. This is a dimensionless model study; I am not claiming it predicts a particular material without calibration.'),
 ('1:15-1:55 | Check the calculation','Robin benchmark: run the three-space-level study.','To test the boundary implementation, I use a heat-equation mode with a known exact solution. I hold the time step fixed and refine the grid from sixteen to thirty-two to sixty-four cells. The measured errors decrease, and the observed order is approximately two. A separate balance audit compares the thermal-content change with the input through the walls. Neither a smooth animation nor a small algebraic residual is used as an accuracy certificate.'),
 ('1:55-2:35 | Compare a family of experiments','Show the cooling parameter map; select one cell and replay.','Now I turn one run into a parameter question: how much heat remains after a fixed time? Each map cell stores a real numerical trajectory. I can choose a mean, a local probe or a threshold event. Failed runs stay visible, and an event that was not observed is left missing. This is a finite-time parameter scan, not a bifurcation diagram.'),
 ('2:35-3:15 | The original research connection','Front laboratory: play disturbance map and point to norm curves.','The second case comes from my earlier work on a fourth-order travelling front. PhaseAtlas retains that mathematical structure while letting me distinguish motion from deformation. Global, local and weighted norms can behave differently. The general Studio also lets a researcher specify two linear boundary conditions at each end for supported fourth-order equations. These calculations support research questions; they do not prove nonlinear stability.'),
 ('3:15-3:55 | Reuse and contribution','Evidence export, article and reproducibility download.','The contribution is a connected workflow: editable equations, visual experiments, numerical checks and a portable research record. The present suite passes ninety mathematical and numerical tests, with an independent Python reference for selected problems. Computation runs on the user\'s device. I used AI assistance in development, while the solver itself is deterministic and needs no language-model API. The package identifies its pre-event baseline so later hackathon contributions can be recorded honestly. I am Niu Te from Imperial College London. This is PhaseAtlas: from PDE animations to reproducible numerical evidence.')
]
SCRIPT2=[
 ('0:00-0:25','Show homepage cooling animation.','A persuasive PDE animation does not tell us whether a computation is resolved. PhaseAtlas connects an editable model to visual experiments, numerical checks and an exportable research record. I am Niu Te from Imperial College London.'),
 ('0:25-0:55','Show Robin model and refinement chart.','A hot rod exchanging heat with its surroundings gives a practical starting point. Robin boundary data represent wall exchange. A separate exact heat-equation mode lets us test the numerical implementation: on sixteen, thirty-two and sixty-four cells, errors decrease with observed order approximately two. A balance audit exposes the relationship between heat leaving the walls and thermal-content change.'),
 ('0:55-1:25','Show parameter map and original wave animation.','Every cell in a parameter map retains its trajectory and metric definition. A researcher can compare cooling responses and revisit a failed run. The original fourth-order front from my earlier research adds wave-motion and deformation diagnostics, with configurable extra boundary conditions in the general Studio. The numerical evidence is not presented as a stability theorem.'),
 ('1:25-1:55','Show JSON export and article.','Another researcher receives equations, boundaries, arrays and method information, plus selected independent Python checks. The current suite passes ninety tests. The implementation is independent and AI-assisted; simulation runs locally without a runtime language-model API. The dated package separates prior work from contributions made during the event. PhaseAtlas helps turn an interesting picture into an experiment that someone else can inspect.')
]
SOCIAL='''A PDE animation is only the beginning. How do you check the numerical result behind it?

PhaseAtlas connects editable PDEs, animated experiments, controlled refinement and reproducible exports in a browser workbench. Two examples link practical boundary cooling with my earlier research on a fourth-order travelling front.

The current Robin eigenmode benchmark shows approximately second-order spatial convergence. Researchers can compare parameter-dependent responses, inspect boundary balance, and separate wave motion from deformation. Failed or unresolved observations remain visible.

Developed with AI assistance; numerical computation runs locally without a runtime LLM API. This is scientific software and numerical evidence, not a new stability theorem or an experimentally calibrated engineering prediction.

Niu Te | Imperial College London
Source: https://github.com/NIU-55383/PhaseAtlas

Publication fields to add after final checks: current demo URL, public video URL, one-page PDF URL, and the actual October 21-22 contribution. The 18 October version is a pre-event baseline.
'''

